#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186228907 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229191 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e829ae4a-7612-5e40-8214-aa56027c38be" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating Under the Integral Sign"}] :question/id #uuid "301e7fe6-6892-5e0a-9368-b3857ae8fa39" :question/math-academy-id "e-21649" :question/problem "Given that $\\displaystyle J(t)=\\int_{0}^{1} (\\cos t+2x)^2\\, \\textrm{d}x,$ calculate $J'(t)$ for $t\\in\\mathbb R$ by differentiating under the integral sign." :question/worked-solution "Suppose $f(x,t)$ is continuous on $R = [a,b]\\times [c,d]$ and $\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial t}$ exists and is continuous on $R.$ Then, the function\n\n$$\nF(t) = \\int_{a}^b f(x,t)\\,\\textrm{d} x\n$$\n\nis differentiable on $(c,d),$ and\n\n$$\nF'(t) = \\dfrac{\\textrm d}{\\textrm d t}\\int_{a}^b f(x,t)\\,\\textrm{d} x\n=\n\\int_{a}^b \\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial t}(x,t)\\,\\textrm{d} x.\n$$\n\nThis is known as differentiating under the integral sign.\n\nWe wish to compute $J'(t),$ where\n\n$$\nJ(t)=\\int_{0}^{1} (\\cos t+2x)^2\\, \\textrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating under the integral sign, we get\n\n$$\nJ'(t) = \\int_{0}^{1} \\frac{\\partial}{\\partial t}\\Bigl[(\\cos t+2x)^2\\Bigr]\\,\\textrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nNext, let's compute the partial derivative with respect to $t$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂t}[(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+2x)^{2}] & =2(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+2x)\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+2x) \\\\  & =2(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+2x)\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t) \\\\  & =-2(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+2x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this back into our integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{J}^{′}(t) & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}-2(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+2x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t{\\int }_{0}^{1}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+2x)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, let's evaluate the remaining integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+2x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\cdot x{|}_{0}^{1}+{x}^{2}{|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nJ'(t) =-2\\sin t\\,(\\cos t+1).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186228922 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229192 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bbefb3bb-4783-5d77-b404-1f43ee8765de" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Cases Where Differentiating Under the Integral Sign Applies"}] :question/id #uuid "830924ac-a51c-505a-b5b4-c8a4ad916dfe" :question/math-academy-id "e-21732" :question/problem "For which of the following functions is it valid to differentiate under the integral sign?\n\nI. $\\displaystyle F_1(t) = \\int_0^1 t\\cos x\\,\\textrm{d} x, \\quad t \\in \\mathbb R$\n\nII. $\\displaystyle F_2(t) = \\int_0^1 \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{|x - t|}}\\,\\textrm{d} x, \\quad t \\in (0,1)$\n\nIII. $\\displaystyle F_3(t) = \\int_0^1 \\dfrac{1}{1 + (x - t)^2}\\,\\textrm{d} x, \\quad t \\in [0,1]$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose $f(x,t)$ is continuous on $R = [a,b]\\times [c,d]$ and $\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial t}$ exists and is continuous on $R.$ Then, the function\n\n$$\nF(t) = \\int_{a}^b f(x,t)\\,\\textrm{d} x\n$$\n\nis differentiable on $(c,d),$ and\n\n$$\nF'(t) = \\dfrac{\\textrm d}{\\textrm d t}\\int_{a}^b f(x,t)\\,\\textrm{d} x\n=\n\\int_{a}^b \\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial t}(x,t)\\,\\textrm{d} x.\n$$\n\nThis is known as *differentiating under the integral sign.*\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each function:\n\n- For the first function, we have \n\n$$\nf_1(x,t) = t\\cos x.\n$$\n\nWe fix $t_0 \\in \\mathbb R$ and choose $c < t_0 < d$ such that $[c,d] \\subseteq \\mathbb R,$ so $R = [0,1] \\times [c,d].$\n\nNote that $f_1$ is continuous on $R.$ Computing the partial derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial f_1}{\\partial t} = \\cos x\n$$\n\nwhich exists and is continuous on $R.$ Thus, differentiating under the integral sign is valid at $t_0.$ And since our choice of $t_0$ was arbitrary, we can compute $F'_1(t)$ for all real $t.$\n\n- For the second function, we have \n\n$$\nf_2(x,t) = \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{|x - t|}}.\n$$\n\nWe fix $t_0 \\in (0,1)$ and choose $c < t_0 < d$ such that $[c,d] \\subseteq (0,1),$ so $R = [0,1] \\times [c,d].$\n\nNote that $f_2$ is undefined when $x = t,$ since the denominator vanishes. Thus, $f_2$ is not continuous on $R,$ and differentiating under the integral sign is *not* valid.\n\n- For the third function, we have \n\n$$\nf_3(x,t) = \\dfrac{1}{1 + (x - t)^2}.\n$$\n\nWe fix $t_0 \\in (0,1)$ and choose $c < t_0 < d$ such that $[c,d] \\subseteq [0,1],$ so $R = [0,1] \\times [c,d].$\n\nNote that $f_3$ is continuous on $R.$ Computing the partial derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial f_3}{\\partial t} = \\dfrac{2(x - t)}{(1 + (x - t)^2)^2}\n$$\n\nwhich exists and is continuous on $R.$ Thus, differentiating under the integral sign is valid at $t_0.$ And since our choice of $t_0$ was arbitrary, we can compute $F'_3(t)$ for all $t \\in [0,1].$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186435668 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229191 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e829ae4a-7612-5e40-8214-aa56027c38be" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating Under the Integral Sign"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fba7ad86-3a5a-50af-8227-5ad1c1f14e0e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "40+4t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435663}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fba7ad86-3a5a-50af-8227-5ad1c1f14e0e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "40+4t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435663} :answer-field/id #uuid "3a8abd96-4c9a-53da-b4f4-8bc3009cd780" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435665}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c52ae607-3210-5388-a941-db6a09d117b2" :question/math-academy-id "q-334938" :question/problem "Given that $J(t)={\\int }_{1}^{3}(5x+t)^{2}\\,\\text{d}x,$ calculate ${J}^{′}(t)$ for $t\\in R$ by differentiating under the integral sign.\n\n${J}^{′}(t)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Suppose $f(x,t)$ is continuous on $R=[a,b]\\times [c,d]$ and $\\frac{∂f}{∂t}$ exists and is continuous on $R.$ Then, the function\n\n$$\nF(t)={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nis differentiable on $(c,d),$ and\n\n$$\n{F}^{′}(t)=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\frac{∂f}{∂t}(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nThis is known as *differentiating under the integral sign.*\n\nWe wish to compute ${J}^{′}(t),$ where\n\n$$\nJ(t)={\\int }_{1}^{3}(5x+t)^{2}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating under the integral sign, we get\n\n$$\n{J}^{′}(t)={\\int }_{1}^{3}\\frac{∂}{∂t}[(5x+t)^{2}]\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nNext, let's compute the partial derivative with respect to $t$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂t}[(5x+t)^{2}] & =2(5x+t)\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂t}(5x+t) \\\\  & =2(5x+t)\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =2(5x+t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this back into our integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{J}^{′}(t) & ={\\int }_{1}^{3}2(5x+t)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{1}^{3}(5x+t)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, let's evaluate the remaining integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{3}(5x+t)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{1}^{3}5x\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{1}^{3}t\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{5{x}^{2}}{2}{|}_{1}^{3}+tx{|}_{1}^{3} \\\\  & =20+2t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{J}^{′}(t) & =2(20+2t) \\\\  & =4(t+10).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229680 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229192 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bbefb3bb-4783-5d77-b404-1f43ee8765de" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Cases Where Differentiating Under the Integral Sign Applies"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0851666c-8df7-5cf0-89e4-6eabe1bd845f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308810} {:answer/id #uuid "8efbc6c8-7a09-5318-accf-461c658f7882" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309889} {:answer/id #uuid "a71ff141-3580-50d7-b5f2-6668d6c7b8e2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310097} {:answer/id #uuid "d275b645-6617-507d-bf7b-a25f3feb8e55" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310442} {:answer/id #uuid "e829515a-854f-5512-b340-959018c78826" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310591}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e829515a-854f-5512-b340-959018c78826" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310591} :answer-field/id #uuid "2fb11334-8c6e-5325-98d2-c4b442302509" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310918}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c1cf14fc-3ea5-5845-8a1e-89cdf998a2cc" :question/math-academy-id "q-335237" :question/problem "For which of the following functions is it valid to differentiate under the integral sign?\n\nI. ${F}_{1}(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}{t}^{2}x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(tx)\\,\\text{d}x,\\,t\\in R$\n\nII. ${F}_{2}(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{(x-t)^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}x,\\,t\\in R$\n\nIII. ${F}_{3}(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{x}{x-t}\\,\\text{d}x,\\,t<0$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose $f(x,t)$ is continuous on $R=[a,b]\\times [c,d]$ and $\\frac{∂f}{∂t}$ exists and is continuous on $R.$ Then, the function\n\n$$\nF(t)={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nis differentiable on $(c,d),$ and\n\n$$\n{F}^{′}(t)=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\frac{∂f}{∂t}(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nThis is known as *differentiating under the integral sign.*\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each function:\n\n- For the first function, we have \n\n$$\n{f}_{1}(x,t)={t}^{2}x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(tx).\n$$\n\nWe fix ${t}_{0}\\in R$ and choose $c<{t}_{0}<d,$ so $R=[0,1]\\times [c,d].$\n\nNote that ${f}_{1}$ is continuous on $R.$ Computing the partial derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂t}=2tx\\operatorname{cos}⁡(tx)-{t}^{2}{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(tx)\n$$\n\nwhich exists and is continuous on $R.$ Thus, differentiating under the integral sign is valid at ${t}_{0}.$ And since our choice of ${t}_{0}$ was arbitrary, we can compute ${F}_{1}^{′}(t)$ for all real $t.$\n\n- For the second function, we have \n\n$$\n{f}_{2}(x,t)=\\frac{1}{(x-t)^{2}+1}.\n$$\n\nWe fix ${t}_{0}\\in R$ and choose $c<{t}_{0}<d,$ so $R=[0,1]\\times [c,d].$\n\nNote that ${f}_{2}$ is continuous on $R$ because the denominator is always positive. Computing the partial derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂t}=\\frac{2(x-t)}{{((x-t)^{2}+1)}^{2}}\n$$\n\nwhich exists and is continuous on $R.$ Thus, differentiating under the integral sign is valid at ${t}_{0}.$ And since our choice of ${t}_{0}$ was arbitrary, we can compute ${F}_{2}^{′}(t)$ for all $t\\in R.$\n\n- For the third function, we have \n\n$$\n{f}_{3}(x,t)=\\frac{x}{x-t}.\n$$\n\nWe fix ${t}_{0}<0$ and choose $[c,d]⊂(-\\infty ,0)$ such that $c<{t}_{0}<d,$ so $R=[0,1]\\times [c,d].$\n\nNote that for $t<0$ and $x\\in [0,1],$ the denominator $x-t$ is positive, so ${f}_{3}$ is continuous on $R.$ Computing the partial derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂{f}_{3}}{∂t}=\\frac{x}{(x-t)^{2}}\n$$\n\nwhich exists and is continuous on $R.$ Thus, differentiating under the integral sign is valid at ${t}_{0}.$ And since our choice of ${t}_{0}$ was arbitrary, we can compute ${F}_{3}^{′}(t)$ for all $t<0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311549 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229192 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bbefb3bb-4783-5d77-b404-1f43ee8765de" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Cases Where Differentiating Under the Integral Sign Applies"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "489cf97e-6c0f-5597-800e-5df4c84dc7c6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309302} {:answer/id #uuid "52f463cd-7b7b-5861-a0e6-e446661d097c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309377} {:answer/id #uuid "5e89d5e4-0385-57c3-bb82-acd44dba8514" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309455} {:answer/id #uuid "a477c7ea-2183-5af4-afc2-1c9de12376cd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310073} {:answer/id #uuid "ad7cc0b7-396a-5b5c-b805-605879748b71" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310152}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a477c7ea-2183-5af4-afc2-1c9de12376cd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310073} :answer-field/id #uuid "27253de7-dbc7-5838-a4cb-f5a95a31f6a3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310892}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0a8a1841-dc47-5a84-bd2d-e5c201943d9f" :question/math-academy-id "q-335246" :question/problem "For which of the following functions is it valid to differentiate under the integral sign?\n\nI. ${F}_{1}(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt[3]{|x-t|}}\\,\\text{d}x,\\,t\\in (0,1)$\n\nII. ${F}_{2}(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{x}{1+{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x,\\,t\\in R$\n\nIII. ${F}_{3}(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{1+x+t}\\,\\text{d}x,\\,t\\in (0,1)$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose $f(x,t)$ is continuous on $R=[a,b]\\times [c,d]$ and $\\frac{∂f}{∂t}$ exists and is continuous on $R.$ Then, the function\n\n$$\nF(t)={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nis differentiable on $(c,d),$ and\n\n$$\n{F}^{′}(t)=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\frac{∂f}{∂t}(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nThis is known as *differentiating under the integral sign.*\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each function:\n\n- For the first function, we have \n\n$$\n{f}_{1}(x,t)=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt[3]{|x-t|}}.\n$$\n\nWe fix ${t}_{0}\\in (0,1)$ and choose $c<{t}_{0}<d$ such that $[c,d]⊆(0,1),$ so $R=[0,1]\\times [c,d].$\n\nNote that ${f}_{1}$ is undefined when $x=t,$ since the denominator vanishes. Thus, ${f}_{1}$ is not continuous on $R,$ and differentiating under the integral sign is *not* valid.\n\n- For the second function, we have \n\n$$\n{f}_{2}(x,t)=\\frac{x}{1+{t}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nWe fix ${t}_{0}\\in R$ and choose $c<{t}_{0}<d,$ so $R=[0,1]\\times [c,d].$\n\nNote that ${f}_{2}$ is continuous on $R.$ Computing the partial derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂t}=-\\frac{2tx}{(1+{t}^{2})^{2}}\n$$\n\nwhich exists and is continuous on $R.$ Thus, differentiating under the integral sign is valid at ${t}_{0}.$ And since our choice of ${t}_{0}$ was arbitrary, we can compute ${F}_{2}^{′}(t)$ for all real $t.$\n\n- For the third function, we have \n\n$$\n{f}_{3}(x,t)=\\frac{1}{1+x+t}.\n$$\n\nWe fix ${t}_{0}\\in (0,1)$ and choose $c<{t}_{0}<d$ such that $[c,d]⊂(0,1),$ so $R=[0,1]\\times [c,d].$\n\nNote that ${f}_{3}$ is continuous on $R$ (since the denominator never vanishes on $R$). Computing the partial derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂{f}_{3}}{∂t}=-\\frac{1}{(1+x+t)^{2}}\n$$\n\nwhich exists and is continuous on $R.$ Thus, differentiating under the integral sign is valid at ${t}_{0}.$ And since our choice of ${t}_{0}$ was arbitrary, we can compute ${F}_{3}^{′}(t)$ for all $t\\in (0,1).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186435667 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229191 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e829ae4a-7612-5e40-8214-aa56027c38be" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating Under the Integral Sign"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fccadc68-ba31-5689-a5be-fac6a54a34ea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}+\\frac{2}{3}e^{2t}+e^{4t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435664}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fccadc68-ba31-5689-a5be-fac6a54a34ea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}+\\frac{2}{3}e^{2t}+e^{4t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435664} :answer-field/id #uuid "9450d3d5-ae63-5af3-bb4d-54253eb9dcc6" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435666}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a0a5fd86-1340-5688-ba59-8ec4e8026e82" :question/math-academy-id "q-352881" :question/problem "Given that $I(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}({x}^{2}+{e}^{2t})^{3}\\,\\text{d}x,$ calculate ${I}^{′}(t)$ for $t\\in R$ by differentiating under the integral sign.\n\n${I}^{′}(t)=6{e}^{2t}(\\,$\n{{field-1}}$)$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose $f(x,t)$ is continuous on $R=[a,b]\\times [c,d]$ and $\\frac{∂f}{∂t}$ exists and is continuous on $R.$ Then, the function\n\n$$\nF(t)={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nis differentiable on $(c,d),$ and\n\n$$\n{F}^{′}(t)=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\frac{∂f}{∂t}(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nThis is known as *differentiating under the integral sign.*\n\nWe wish to compute ${I}^{′}(t),$ where\n\n$$\nI(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}({x}^{2}+{e}^{2t})^{3}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating under the integral sign, we get\n\n$$\n{I}^{′}(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{∂}{∂t}[({x}^{2}+{e}^{2t})^{3}]\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nNext, let's compute the partial derivative with respect to $t$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂t}[({x}^{2}+{e}^{2t})^{3}] & =3({x}^{2}+{e}^{2t})^{2}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂t}({x}^{2}+{e}^{2t}) \\\\  & =3({x}^{2}+{e}^{2t})^{2}\\cdot 2{e}^{2t} \\\\  & =6{e}^{2t}({x}^{2}+{e}^{2t})^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this back into our integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}^{′}(t) & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}6{e}^{2t}({x}^{2}+{e}^{2t})^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =6{e}^{2t}{\\int }_{0}^{1}({x}^{2}+{e}^{2t})^{2}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, let's evaluate the remaining integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}({x}^{2}+{e}^{2t})^{2}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}({x}^{4}+2{x}^{2}{e}^{2t}+{e}^{4t})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{4}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{1}2{x}^{2}{e}^{2t}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{4t}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{5}}{5}{|}_{0}^{1}+{e}^{2t}\\cdot \\frac{2{x}^{3}}{3}{|}_{0}^{1}+{e}^{4t}\\cdot x{|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}+\\frac{2}{3}{e}^{2t}+{e}^{4t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{I}^{′}(t)=6{e}^{2t}(\\frac{1}{5}+\\frac{2}{3}{e}^{2t}+{e}^{4t}).\n$$"}]}
